body:
`canHandle()` doesn't verify that the cube is actually registered/available via `ctx.getCube()`. If `queryCapabilities()` returns `objectqlAggregate: true` for an unknown cube, this strategy will be selected and `execute()` will crash on `ctx.getCube(query.cube!)!`. Consider returning false when `ctx.getCube(query.cube)` is undefined, and have `execute()` throw a clear "Cube not found" error instead of relying on non-null assertions (this also allows fallback strategies to run for unknown cubes).
```suggestion
    if (!caps.objectqlAggregate || typeof ctx.executeAggregate !== 'function') {
      return false;
    }
    if (typeof ctx.getCube !== 'function') {
      return false;
    }
    const cube = ctx.getCube(query.cube);
    if (!cube) {
      return false;
    }
    return true;
  }

  async execute(query: AnalyticsQuery, ctx: StrategyContext): Promise<AnalyticsResult> {
    if (!query.cube) {
      throw new Error('Cube not specified in analytics query');
    }
    if (typeof ctx.getCube !== 'function') {
      throw new Error('Cube registry is not available in strategy context');
    }
    const cube = ctx.getCube(query.cube);
    if (!cube) {
      throw new Error(`Cube not found: ${query.cube}`);
    }
```

`convertFilter()` is emitting Mongo/Mingo-style operators like `$regex` and returning raw values, but ObjectQL filter conventions in this repo use `$contains`/`$notContains` (and then drivers normalize as needed). Using `$regex` directly is likely to break on non-memory drivers, and returning `undefined` can leave `filter[field]=undefined` while still sending a non-empty filter object. Suggest mapping to the canonical operators (`$eq`, `$ne`, `$gt`, `$gte`, `$lt`, `$lte`, `$contains`, `$notContains`, etc.) and skipping filters that have no effective value.
```suggestion
    // Canonical "is set" / "is not set" semantics
    if (operator === 'set') return { $ne: null };
    if (operator === 'notSet') return { $eq: null };

    // No effective filter if there are no values; let caller decide whether to skip
    if (!values || values.length === 0) return undefined;

    switch (operator) {
      case 'equals':
        return { $eq: values[0] };
      case 'notEquals':
        return { $ne: values[0] };
      case 'gt':
        return { $gt: values[0] };
      case 'gte':
        return { $gte: values[0] };
      case 'lt':
        return { $lt: values[0] };
      case 'lte':
        return { $lte: values[0] };
      case 'contains':
        return { $contains: values[0] };
      case 'notContains':
        return { $notContains: values[0] };
      default:
        // Unknown operator: no filter emitted; caller should skip
        return undefined;
```

`extractObjectName()` returns `cube.sql.trim()`, but `CubeSchema.sql` is documented as "Base SQL statement or Table Name". For the ObjectQL aggregate path, the engine typically expects an ObjectQL object name (likely `cube.name`), not an arbitrary SQL string. If `cube.sql` is a SELECT/subquery or differs from `cube.name`, `executeAggregate()` will be invoked with an invalid object name.
```suggestion
  private extractObjectName(cube: Cube): string {
    // Prefer the logical cube name for ObjectQL object resolution.
    if (cube.name && cube.name.trim().length > 0) {
      return cube.name.trim();
    }
    // Fallback to the SQL/table name only if no explicit name is provided.
```

`canHandle()` doesn't check that the cube exists in `ctx.getCube()`. If `queryCapabilities()` returns `nativeSql: true` for an unregistered cube, this strategy will be selected and then crash on `ctx.getCube(query.cube!)!` (and prevent fallback strategies from running). Consider returning false when `ctx.getCube(query.cube)` is undefined and making `execute()` throw a clear "Cube not found" error when needed.
```suggestion

    const cube = ctx.getCube(query.cube);
    if (!cube) return false;

    const caps = ctx.queryCapabilities(query.cube);
    return Boolean(caps.nativeSql && typeof ctx.executeRawSql === 'function');
  }

  async execute(query: AnalyticsQuery, ctx: StrategyContext): Promise<AnalyticsResult> {
    const { sql, params } = await this.generateSql(query, ctx);

    if (!query.cube) {
      throw new Error('Cube not specified in analytics query');
    }

    const cube = ctx.getCube(query.cube);
    if (!cube) {
      throw new Error(`Cube not found: ${query.cube}`);
    }
```

`debug` mode registers an `analytics:beforeQuery` hook, but there are no `ctx.trigger('analytics:beforeQuery', ...)` call sites in the repo. As a result this hook never runs and the option is effectively dead. Consider either removing this hook registration or adding an explicit trigger in the analytics request path (e.g., wherever `IAnalyticsService.query()` is invoked) so the debug logging actually executes.
```suggestion
      ctx.logger.debug('[Analytics] Debug mode enabled');
      const cubeNames = this.options.cubes?.map((cube) => cube.name) ?? [];
      ctx.logger.debug('[Analytics] Registered cubes', { cubes: cubeNames });
```

`canHandle()` returns true when `caps.inMemory` is true even if `ctx.fallbackService` is missing, but `execute()` will then throw "No fallback analytics service available". This makes the strategy claim it can handle a query when it cannot. Consider making `canHandle()` require `ctx.fallbackService` (or alternatively implement a non-fallback execution path for true in-memory drivers).
```suggestion
    // Strategy can only operate when a fallback service is available
    const caps = ctx.queryCapabilities(query.cube);
    // Prefer in-memory drivers, but always require a fallback service
    if (caps.inMemory && ctx.fallbackService) return true;
    // Fallback-only path (preserves existing behavior when a fallback is registered)
    return !!ctx.fallbackService;
```
